<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Selonians</h1><p class="st-meta">600 CE – 1208 CE (approx.) · capital Sēlpils</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Small Baltic people on the south bank of the Daugava, the first of the Latvian tribes to fall to the German crusaders in 1208.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Selonians settled in what is now south-eastern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-latvia">Latvia</a> and north-eastern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Lithuania</a> during the first millennium, between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/semigallians">Semigallians</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latgalians">Latgalians</a>. Their name survives in the region of Sēlija. They lived in communities centred on hillforts, without a single ruler.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Archaeology shows farming villages, fortified hills and graves rich in bronze ornaments. Their position on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-dvina">Daugava</a> trade route brought them under the influence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polotsk">Polotsk</a> and, by the 12th century, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a>, who used their main fortress, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selpils">Sēlpils</a>, as a base for raids.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1208 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-of-riga">Bishop Albert of Riga</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/livonian-brothers-of-the-sword">Livonian Brothers of the Sword</a> besieged and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selpils">Sēlpils</a>, and the Selonians accepted baptism. Their land was later divided between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/livonian-order">Livonian Order</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/archbishopric-of-riga">Archbishopric of Riga</a>, and a short-lived <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bishopric-of-semigallia">Bishopric of Selonia</a> was founded. The Selonians merged into the Latvian and Lithuanian peoples.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/selonians.jpg" alt="Selonians" width="320" height="353"><figcaption>Image: MapMaster · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baltic_Tribes_c_1200.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>600 CE – 1208 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sēlpils</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Baltic paganism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Selonian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Latvia, Lithuania</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Selonia, Sēlija</dd></dl></aside></div>

